1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,148
Here's another really smart human trick suppose I ask you how many penguins are there.
这里还有一个人类很聪明的小技巧：假设我问你，这里有多少只企鹅？

2
00:00:04,846 --> 00:00:10,851
Well I bet I know how you'd solve that problem if you solved it you went one two three four five six seven eight.
我敢说我知道你会怎样解决这个问题：如果你算出来了，就是数着一、二、三、四、五、六、七、八。

3
00:00:10,875 --> 00:00:16,332
You counted them you named each one with a number and the last number you said was the number of penguins.
你把它们数了一遍，给每一只配上一个数字；最后说出的数字，就是企鹅的数量。

4
00:00:16,356 --> 00:00:19,218
This is a little trick that you're taught to use as kids.
这是你们小时候就被教会使用的小技巧。

5
00:00:19,242 --> 00:00:24,121
You learn the number list and you learn how to apply it a little linguistic trick.
你学习数字序列，再学习怎样应用它；这是一个小小的语言技巧。

6
00:00:24,692 --> 00:00:29,542
Well some languages don't do this because some languages don't have exact number words.
她说，有些语言不这样做，因为有些语言没有表示精确数字的词。

7
00:00:29,927 --> 00:00:34,249
They're languages that don't have a word like seven or a word like eight.
有些语言没有像“七”或“八”这样的词。

8
00:00:34,921 --> 00:00:40,600
In fact people who speak these languages don't count and they have trouble keeping track of exact quantities.
她进一步说，说这些语言的人不计数，也难以追踪精确数量。

9
00:00:40,624 --> 00:00:46,841
So for example if I ask you to match this number of penguins to the same number of ducks.
比如，如果我让你用同样数量的鸭子，匹配这里的企鹅数量。

10
00:00:46,865 --> 00:00:52,888
You would be able to do that by counting but folks who don't have that linguistic trick can't do that.
你能够通过计数完成；但她说，没有这种语言技巧的人无法这样完成。

11
00:00:55,541 --> 00:00:59,869
Languages also differ in how they divide up the color spectrum the visual world.
语言也在如何划分色谱、划分视觉世界这方面有所不同。

12
00:01:00,236 --> 00:01:04,776
Some languages have lots of words for colors some have only a couple words light and dark.
有些语言有许多颜色词；有些只有少数几个，例如明与暗。

13
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:08,423
And languages differ in where they put boundaries between colors.
不同语言为颜色划定分界的位置也不同。

14
00:01:08,447 --> 00:01:14,821
So for example in english there's a word for blue that covers all of the colors that you can see on the screen.
比如，在英语里，有一个表示蓝色的词，可以涵盖屏幕上你们看到的所有这些颜色。

15
00:01:14,845 --> 00:01:22,176
But in russian there isn't a single word instead russian speakers have to differentiate between light blue goluboy and dark blue siniy.
但她说，俄语里没有一个单独的词统括这些颜色；说俄语的人要区分浅蓝色 goluboy 和深蓝色 siniy。

16
00:01:23,026 --> 00:01:28,902
So russians have this lifetime of experience of in language distinguishing these two colors.
因此，她认为，俄语使用者一生都在通过语言区分这两种颜色。

17
00:01:28,926 --> 00:01:37,016
When we test people's ability to perceptually discriminate these colors what we find is that russian speakers are faster across this linguistic boundary.
当我们测试人们从感知上辨别这些颜色的能力时，她说，俄语使用者在跨越这一语言分类界线时反应更快。

18
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:40,804
They're faster to be able to tell the difference between a light and dark blue.
他们能够更快地区分浅蓝与深蓝。

19
00:01:41,187 --> 00:01:47,810
And when you look at people's brains as they're looking at colors say you have colors shifting slowly from light to dark blue.
再看看人们观察颜色时的大脑活动：比方说，颜色慢慢从浅蓝变成深蓝。

20
00:01:48,686 --> 00:01:56,672
The brains of people who use different words for light and dark blue will give a surprised reaction as the colors shift from light to dark.
她说，使用不同词语表示浅蓝与深蓝的人，在颜色由浅变深时，大脑会出现一种“惊讶”反应。

21
00:01:56,696 --> 00:01:59,878
As if ooh something has categorically changed.
就好像在说：“噢，某个东西在类别上变了。”

22
00:01:59,902 --> 00:02:07,839
Whereas the brains of english speakers for example that don't make this categorical distinction don't give that surprise because nothing is categorically changing.
相比之下，她说，比如不作这种类别区分的英语使用者，其大脑没有这种惊讶反应，因为类别并没有变化。

23
00:02:09,942 --> 00:02:13,807
Languages have all kinds of structural quirks this is one of my favorites.
语言还有各种结构上的特别之处；这是我最喜欢的例子之一。

24
00:02:13,831 --> 00:02:20,917
Lots of languages have grammatical gender every noun gets assigned a gender often masculine or feminine.
很多语言有语法性别，每个名词都会被赋予一个性别，通常是阳性或阴性。

25
00:02:20,941 --> 00:02:28,935
And these genders differ across languages so for example the sun is feminine in german but masculine in spanish and the moon the reverse.
而不同语言的这些分类也不一样；比如“太阳”在德语里是阴性，在西班牙语里是阳性，“月亮”则正好相反。

26
00:02:29,522 --> 00:02:32,984
Could this actually have any consequence for how people think.
这真的会对人们怎样思考产生任何影响吗？

27
00:02:33,008 --> 00:02:38,932
Do german speakers think of the sun as somehow more female like and the moon somehow more male like.
说德语的人会不会觉得太阳更像女性，而月亮更像男性呢？

28
00:02:39,655 --> 00:02:48,468
Actually it turns out that's the case so if you ask german and spanish speakers to say describe a bridge like the one here.
她说，事实结果确实如此；比如，请德语和西班牙语使用者描述一座像这里这样的桥。

29
00:02:48,492 --> 00:02:54,021
Bridge happens to be grammatically feminine in german grammatically masculine in spanish.
“桥”恰好在德语里属于语法阴性，在西班牙语里属于语法阳性。

30
00:02:54,045 --> 00:03:00,514
German speakers are more likely to say bridges are beautiful elegant and stereotypically feminine words.
她说，德语使用者更可能用“美丽”“优雅”等在刻板印象中偏女性化的词描述桥。

31
00:03:00,538 --> 00:03:09,337
Whereas spanish speakers will be more likely to say they're strong or long these masculine words laughter.
而她说，西班牙语使用者更可能用“强壮”“长”等她称为偏男性化的词。（观众笑）
